Figure 49 Here, we visually see the procedure known as the invariant-mass method. On the left panel, a second-degree polynomial fits the background (in green) and two summed Gaussian distributions fit the signal (in red). The ratios of these fits give \(f^{\rm sig.}(m_{\rm inv.})\) (in blue) and \(f^{\rm bgd.}(m_{\rm inv.})\) (in black). On the right panel, we see those used to fit \(\left\langle\sin(\Psi_{1}-\phi_{p}^{*})\right\rangle\) with Eq. 25. It is apparent here that the fit function is not appropriate for the data, and that this method is invalid in this situation.

Figure 50 Polarization as a function of rapidity using the standard (and in this case invalid) method; we see dramatic behavior such that the polarization increases strongly with \(y\), and the statistical uncertainties are such that we would quote such a dependence with certainty.

Figure 51 Polarization as a function of \(p_{\rm T}\) using the standard (and in this case invalid) method; we see dramatic behavior such that the polarization increases strongly with \(p_{\rm T}\) before sharply dropping off, and the statistical uncertainties are such that we would quote such a dependence with certainty.

