## 3 Global \(\Lambda\) polarization in Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=3\) GeV

### Introduction

In relativistic heavy-ion collisions, there is substantial angular momentum that depends on \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\); for the energy range spanned by RHIC, \(|\vec{J}_{\rm sys.}|\sim{\cal O}(1-100\frac{\rm TeV~{}fm}{c})\). The majority of this remains in the spectators and forward-going particles while a fraction is transferred to the QGP (see Fig. 65). If the Lorentz-contracted nuclei were disks of uniform thickness this would not be true; however, due to the radially decreasing density profile (as seen as a projection in the transverse plane in Fig. 67) one "side" of the QGP carries more forward-going particles than the other, and there is a net angular momentum in the QGP (defined using "participant" notation, \(\vec{J}_{\rm part}\)). Via spin-orbit coupling, this \(\vec{J}_{\rm part}\) is transferred in part to the spin polarization of partons within the QGP. Due to angular-momentum conservation, \(\vec{J}_{\rm part}\) will be transferred in part to the spin polarization of hadrons.

To measure this experimentally, one would want to correlate the direction of global hadron spin, \(\hat{S}\), with \(\hat{J}_{\rm syst}\) (though \(\hat{J}_{\rm part}\) may seem a more natural choice; this is discussed later, in Sec. 4). While measuring \(\hat{S}\) of a generic particle would be quite difficult experimentally, some particles reveal the direction of their spins by preferential "decay orientations". The most straightforwardparticle to use is the \(\Lambda\) hyperon via the decay channel \(\Lambda\to p^{+}+\pi^{-}\) which accounts for the majority of \(\Lambda\)-hyperon decays. The proton is emitted preferentially along the direction of the \(\Lambda\) hyperon's spin, and the corresponding decay parameter is \(\alpha_{\Lambda}=0.732\pm 0.014\)[69]. Recall that the reaction plane is spanned by \(\hat{b}\times\hat{p}_{\rm beam}\) and is orthogonal to \(\vec{J}_{\rm syst}\); the angle it makes with \(\hat{x}\) in the detector system is the reaction-plane angle, \(\Psi_{\rm RP}\). This is estimated by the first-order event-plane anlge, \(\Psi_{1}\), and the resolution, \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\), describes how well the two are correlated. For a symmetric detector and colliding system, we may write the polarization as

\[\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\equiv\left\langle\vec{P}_{\Lambda}\cdot\hat{J}_{\rm sys.}\right\rangle=\frac{8}{\pi\alpha_{\Lambda}}\frac{1}{R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}}\left \langle\sin\left(\Psi_{\rm EP,1}-\phi_{p}^{*}\right)\right\rangle\right)^{\rm sig}. \tag{7}\]

In a publication by the STAR collaboration in 2007 at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=62.4,200\) GeV, this was measured to be consistent with zero; however, studying the effect across a range of lower \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\) demonstrated a rising \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) with falling \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\) (see Fig. 36). Later, higher-statistics data sets at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=200\) GeV by the STAR collaboration [50] and later at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=2.76,5.02\) GeV by the ALICE collaboration [4] demonstrated \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}<0.5\%\).

The trend of falling \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) with \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\) is convincing, and one wonders how low in \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\) the trend can continue. At \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=2m_{\rm N}\), \(|\vec{J}_{\rm syst}|\) is zero and therefore so must \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\). Very recent theoretical calculations find a peak \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) near \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\approx 3\) GeV [31, 24]. This study aims to achieve the experimental measure of \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=3\) GeV.

### Event and \(\Lambda\) selection

#### Event selection

In order to achieve the collision energy of \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=3\) GeV, the "yellow" ion storage ring (carrying ions counter-clockwise around RHIC when viewed from above and eastward at STAR's location on the south side of RHIC) held ions with nucleon energy 3.85 GeV and was steered downward by a few centimeters to hit a thin Au foil positioned at \(z=200\) cm (see Fig. 37). An "event" was recorded if there was an above-threshold signal in both the East BBC and in the TOF. A total of over a third of a billion events were recorded, although not all recorded events were usable.

The "primary vertex" of an event is determined by tracing the measured particles' tracks back to a common origin (which is not shared by all tracks; e.g. products of decays) and approximates the point of collision. Figure 37 shows the distribution of the primary vertices in the \(y-z\) and \(x-y\) planes; it is obvious that a significant number of events occurred as a result of beam interactions with the beam pipe and with the fixed-target support structure. In order to filter these events out, we simply impose appropriate "cuts", or selections, on \(v_{z}\) and \(v_{\rm T}=\sqrt{v_{x}^{2}+(v_{y}-2\ {\rm cm})^{2}}\)

Figure 37: A slide from [48], demonstrating the Au target’s position.

Additional cuts are imposed on events with too many "primary tracks" (those whose paths can be traced to within 3 cm of the primary vertex) which correspond to "pileup" events (in which the tracks from multiple collisions were recorded), events that were marked as "bad" by the operators at the time of data taking (for various reasons), and events which were later found to have other problems. In order to maintain consistency across analysis performed on a given data set, standard practice dictates that a dedicated group of people build these qualifications for and lists of bad runs. The experimental nuclear physics group at the University of California at Davis performed these studies; further details can be found in appendix A.1

### \(\Lambda\) selection

We are interested in measuring \(\overline{P}_{\rm H}\) using \(\Lambda\) hyperons, through the decay \(\Lambda\to p^{+}+\pi^{-}\). Recall that STAR's magnetic field is aligned with \(-\hat{z}\) and therefore the positively and negatively charged particles curve in opposite directions. Typically [7], one will first identify protons and pions using the track curvatures, energy loss in the TPC, and (if applicable) measured mass using the methods described in Sec. 1.2. Additionally, one will impose a series of quality selections, or "cuts" on each particle. In order to avoid contributions of "spallation" protons originating from beampipe interactions, a standard cut of \(p_{\rm T}\geq 0.4\) GeV/\(c\) is applied to the protons, and to avoid tracking issues associated with low momentum, a cut of \(p_{T}\geq 0.15\) GeV/\(c\) is applied to the pions. In order to neglect very poor position and momentum resolution, a cut on the minimum number of TPC signals associated with a track of \(\geq 15\) is applied.

Then, a series of cuts are applied to each \([p,\pi]\) pair to determine whether they likely originated from a \(\Lambda\) decay. The most obvious is perhaps on the separation between their helical paths; if the measured Distance at Closest Approach (DCA) is larger than, say 1 cm, then the proton and pion probably did not originate from a decay. Furthermore, if the (again, measured) DCA between either of the helical paths and the primary vertex is very small, say less than 1 cm, then the relevant particle likely originated from the primary vertex rather than a decay, later on.

Two final cuts are imposed on the \(\Lambda\) "candidate" reconstructed using the \([p,\pi]\) pair. The DCA of the \(\Lambda\) to the primary vertex must be sufficiently close in order to filter out \(\Lambda\)s that were not directly emitted from the collision (i.e. which were a product of another decay). Finally, the decay length of the \(\Lambda\) must be sufficiently large to neglect the abundance of primary protons and pions (those originating directly from the collision) that would pass the prior cuts more easily.

While we ultimately did perform the above procedure for reconstructing \(\Lambda\) hyperons, we did so only in our studies of systematic effects. For the quoted results, we used a machine-learning-based algorithm which achieves overall better purity, called KFParticle [71, 27]. Details on our usage of KFParticle, as well as the specific cuts used in our \(\Lambda\) reconstruction for the systematics study and further \(\Lambda\) kinematic cuts, can be found in appendixA.2

A reconstructed \(\Lambda\) has a measured invariant mass, \(m_{\rm inv.}\), associated with it. The true \(\Lambda\) mass as quoted by the Particle Data Group (PDG), \(m_{\Lambda,{\rm PDG}}\), is 1.11568 GeV/\(c\); we might then expect to see a distribution of \(m_{\rm inv.}\) from reconstructed \(\Lambda\)s to be peaked about \(m_{\Lambda,{\rm PDG}}\) where a significant fraction of counts on the peak correspond to legitimate \(\Lambda\) decays and the majority of counts outside the peak to be \([p,\pi]\) pairs that coincidentally passed the series of cuts and are falsely reconstructed \(\Lambda\)s (called the "combinatoric background"). Figure 38 demonstrates the \(m_{\rm inv.}\) distributions of \(\Lambda\)s reconstructed using KFParticle as well as the "manual" cuts used for the systematics study.

Figure 38: \(m_{\rm inv.}\) distribution for \(\Lambda\)s found using the traditional method compared to that with those found using the KFParticle package.

### Event-plane determination

#### Measuring \(\Psi_{1}\) with the EPD

Recall the reaction plane, which is spanned by \(\vec{p}_{\rm beam}\times\vec{b}\). Because of finite statistics and detector acceptance, as well as event-by-event fluctuations, it is impossible to know the orientation of the reaction plane, \(\Psi_{\rm RP}\), exactly. We can only estimate \(\Psi_{\rm RP}\) through the azimuthal distribution of emitted particles, which yields an event-plane angle. We are interested in the first-order event-plane angle, \(\Psi_{\rm EP,1}\), which is given as [62]

\[\Psi_{\rm EP,1}={\rm atan2}\left(\sum_{i}w_{i}\sin\phi_{i},\sum_{i}w_{i}\cos \phi_{i}\right), \tag{8}\]

where \({\rm atan2}\) is the C++ function and the sum is carried out over the particles in a chosen region of \(p_{\rm T},y\), etc. In the case of a scintillator detector such as the EPD (used for our determination of \(\Psi_{\rm EP,1}\)), we don't have access to these variables; we can only choose a region in \(\eta\). The choice of weight, \(w_{i}\), varies, but at least should involve \({\rm sign}(y)\). Our precision on \(\phi_{i}\) is limited due to the tile angular width of \(\pi/12\) (or \(\pi/6\) in the case of the inner EPD ring; see Fig. 15), so we choose a random point within the tile (see appendix D). At larger \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\) and when using the EPD for \(\Psi_{\rm EP,1}\) measurement, an additional factor is included: the strength of the measured signal ("ADC"), which is loosely related to the number of particles that had passed through a tile. At \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=3\) GeV, the multi-hit probability is very small, so we do not apply this additional factor to the weight.

In either case, a signal strength must pass a threshold in order to be considered a "hit", to exclude detector "noise". Through fitting performed on the ADC distribution using Landau distributions (carried out separately during the data-calibration process), one can apply this cut in units of "number of minimum-ionizing particles", or nMIP. The advantage of the nMIP units over the ADC units is that they scale with offsets or gains in ADC. The ADC distribution and its Landau fits are shown in Fig. 39 at higher \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\) where multiple peaks are visible. Regardless of \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\), the effects of noise are negligible above nMIP=0.3 and we therefore apply this cut.

Only the east side of the Event-Plane Detector (EPD) is used since essentially no produced particles pass through the west side of the EPD. Because

Figure 39: The nMIP distribution of inner EPD rings along with the multiple Landau fits each corresponding to a different number of MIPs passing through the tile in an event. The data shown is taken at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=27\) GeV where multi-hit probability is large relative to that at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=3\) GeV. Image courtesy of Xiaoyu Liu.

we don't have a symmetric collider/detector system for this data set, we use the three-subevent method for determining \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\), comparing \(\Psi_{1}\) measured using the subevent of choice to \(\Psi_{1}\) measured using reference subevents \(\Psi_{1,{\rm Ref.1}}\) and \(\Psi_{1,{\rm Ref.2}}\)[52]:

\[R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}=\sqrt{\frac{\left\langle\cos(\Psi_{1}-\Psi_{1,{\rm Ref.1}}) \right\rangle\left\langle\cos(\Psi_{1}-\Psi_{1,{\rm Ref.2}})\right\rangle}{ \left\langle\cos(\Psi_{1,{\rm Ref.1}}-\Psi_{1,{\rm Ref.2}})\right\rangle}}. \tag{9}\]

Although we could use the entire east EPD wheel for the measurement of \(\Psi_{1}\), our \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\) would be overestimated because of momentum-conservation effects that are substantial in collisions of low energy (and therefore low multiplicity); see Sec. B.1. As detailed in appendix B.1, only the outer four wheels of the EPD are used for the determination of \(\Psi_{\rm EP,1}\). As detailed in appendix B.2, the two reference subevents are tracks in the TPC with \(-0.5<\eta<-0.4\) and \(-0.2<\eta<-0.1\).

Due to a number of factors, including non-uniformity of detector acceptance, the distribution of \(\Psi_{\rm EP,1}\) will not be completely flat. If these "raw" event-plane angles are used, there will be an overall preference of some orientation. The process involved in forcing a flat distribution, and thereby removing any such preference, is detailed in appendix C. After this procedure, we run through all events again to correlate the \(\Psi_{1}\)s and subsequently calculate \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\) according to Eq. 9.

We see in Figs. 41, 41, 42, and 43 these correlations and the resultant \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\) of the EPD rings used, as a function of collision centrality.

\(\vec{B}_{\rm STAR}\)**-driven \(\Psi_{1,\rm EPD}\) rotation**

As we know, the STAR magnetic field, \(\vec{B}_{\rm STAR}\), runs parallel to the beam thereby bending charged-particle tracks in the transverse plane. This is necessary for crucial TPC measurements, but poses a potential problem when measuring \(\Psi_{1}\) with any detector that lacks tracking; the azimuthal position of a charged particle does not accurately reflect the azimuthal position of that charged particle at its DCA to the primary vertex. This is a simple derivation of the azimuthal angle of relativistic charged particle traveling in a constant magnetic field \(\vec{B}=B\hat{z}\); we start from \(\vec{F}=\gamma m\vec{a}\):

\[\gamma m\frac{d\vec{v}}{dt}=q\vec{v}\times\vec{B}=qB\left(v_{y}\hat{x}-v_{x} \hat{y}\right). \tag{10}\]

Looking at the \(x\) and \(y\) directions separately, and using

\[\vec{\omega}=-\frac{q\vec{B}}{\gamma m},\quad\omega=-\frac{qB}{\gamma m}, \tag{11}\]

we have

\[\frac{dv_{x}}{dt}=-\omega v_{y},\quad\frac{dv_{y}}{dt}=\omega v_{x}, \tag{12}\]

which lead to the familiar

\[\frac{d^{2}v_{x}}{dt^{2}}=-\omega^{2}\frac{dv_{y}}{dt},\quad\frac{d^{2}v_{x}} {dt^{2}}=-\omega^{2}\frac{dv_{y}}{dt}, \tag{13}\]

which have the solutions

\[v_{x}=a_{1}\sin\left(\pm\omega t+b_{1}\right),\quad v_{y}=a_{2}\sin\left(\pm \omega t+b_{2}\right). \tag{14}\]We can consider the fact that the initial velocity in the \(x-y\) plane is given in terms of the initial azimuthal angle \(\phi_{0}\) as

\[v_{x}=|\vec{v}_{\perp}|\cos\left(\phi_{0}\right),\quad v_{y}=|\vec{v}_{\perp}| \sin\left(\phi_{0}\right) \tag{15}\]

along with Eq. 14 taken at \(t\) = 0,

\[v_{x}=a_{1}\cos\left(b_{1}\right),\quad v_{y}=a_{2}\cos\left(b_{2}\right), \tag{16}\]

to see that \(a_{1}=a_{2}=|\vec{v}_{\perp}|\equiv v_{\perp}\), \(b_{1}=\phi_{0}+\pi/2\), and \(b_{2}=\phi_{0}\). Integrating, we then have:

\[x=\frac{v_{\perp}}{\pm\omega}\sin\left(\pm\omega t+\phi_{0}\right)+c_{1}, \quad y=\frac{-v_{\perp}}{\pm\omega}\cos\left(\pm\omega t+\phi_{0}\right)+c_{2}. \tag{17}\]

Taking \(t\) = 0 yields

\[c_{1}=x_{0}-\frac{v_{\perp}}{\pm\omega}\sin\left(\phi_{0}\right),\quad c_{2}= y_{0}+\frac{v_{\perp}}{\pm\omega}\cos\left(\phi_{0}\right), \tag{18}\]

leading to the time-dependent azimuthal angle

\[\begin{split}\phi\left(t\right)=\text{atan2}&\bigg{(} \frac{-v_{\perp}}{\pm\omega}\left[\cos\left(\pm\omega t+\phi_{0}\right)-\cos \left(\phi_{0}\right)\right]+y_{0},\\ &\frac{v_{\perp}}{\pm\omega}\left[\sin\left(\pm\omega t+\phi_{0} \right)-\sin\left(\phi_{0}\right)\right]+x_{0}\bigg{)},\end{split} \tag{19}\]

where atan2(y,x) is the C++ function.

We would like to remove the explicit presence of time. Looking at the direction, we have

\[t=\frac{\Delta z}{v_{z}}=\frac{\gamma m\Delta z}{p_{z}}\quad\Rightarrow\quad \omega t=-\frac{qB\Delta z}{p_{z}}. \tag{20}\]

If we take for simplicity a particle with initial momentum \(\vec{p}=|\vec{p}|\hat{x}\), starting at the origin, we have

\[x=\frac{v_{\perp}}{\pm\omega}\sin\left(\pm\omega t\right),\quad\frac{-v_{\perp }}{\pm\omega}\left[\cos\left(\pm\omega t\right)-1\right]. \tag{21}\]

We can then use the right-hand rule to determine whether we take the positive or negative sign for the \(\omega\), and see that it should be the positive sign. For this simple example, we then have:

\[\phi\left(p_{z}\right)=\text{atan2}\left[1-\cos\left(-\frac{qB\Delta z}{p_{z}} \right),\quad\sin\left(-\frac{qB\Delta z}{p_{z}}\right)\right] \tag{22}\]

For the sake of generality, the momentum-dependent azimuthal angle is

\[\begin{split}\phi\left(p_{z}\right)=\text{atan2}\bigg{(}\frac{-v _{\perp}}{\omega}\left[\cos\left(\omega t+\phi_{0}\right)-\cos\left(\phi_{0} \right)\right]+y_{0},\\ \frac{v_{\perp}}{\omega}\left[\sin\left(\omega t+\phi_{0}\right) -\sin\left(\phi_{0}\right)\right]+x_{0}\bigg{)}.\end{split} \tag{23}\]

We see from Eq. 22 that, surprisingly, the azimuthal rotation of a charged track due to \(\vec{B}_{\text{STAR}}\) is independent of \(p_{\text{T}}\). Intuitively, we might expect \(p_{\text{T}}\) dependence because low-\(p_{\text{T}}\) tracks have higher curvatures, but over a given distance in \(z\), the azimuthal rotation is independent of this curvature. Checks don't hurt though, so Fig. 44 shows Eq. 22 plotted alongside a simulation of the same scenario using StPhysicalHelix, and the two agree.

We also see from Eq. 22 that the collision energy of this analysis presents the worst-case scenario for \(\Psi_{1,\rm EPD}\) rotation; \(\Delta z\), at 2 m, is twice as large as the average in collider mode, \(p_{z}\) is on average smaller than any other RHIC energy reached so far, and forward-rapidity tracks are almost entirely positive (at higher energies, where there is more charge symmetry, positive and negative tracks curl in opposite directions and the net effect is closer to a smearing rather than a rotation). We can measure this effect through the distribution of \(\Psi_{1,\rm TPC}-\phi_{\rm EPDhit}\). The charge asymmetry or average \(p_{z}\) may depend on collision centrality, so we check the centrality dependence; Fig. 46 shows such a distribution. The distributions are fitted with a cosine, and the phase shifts are indicative of the \(\vec{B}_{\rm STAR}\)-induced azimuthal rotation. There is no apparent reason why the extracted phase shift would depend on the TPC subevent chosen, so we use different TPC subevents as a check. Fig. 47 shows the extracted phase shifts (with an additional shift by \(\pi\) as an effective rapidity weight - the

Figure 44: Azimuthal rotation of charged tracks due to \(\vec{B}_{\rm STAR}\) as a function of \(p_{z}\). Eq. 22 and Helix calculations agree.

TPC and EPD lie on opposite sides of mid-rapidity) with respect to collision centrality and TPC subevent of choice. The surprising dependence on TPC subevent is something that indicates a problem; any non-unity \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\) would only impact the amplitude of the cosine fit and any non-flow correlations would only impact \(\langle\cos(\Psi_{1,{\rm TPC}}-\phi_{\rm EPDhit})\rangle\), not \(\langle\sin(\Psi_{1,{\rm TPC}}-\phi_{\rm EPDhit})\rangle\). We can, however, see that this dependence on subevent is dominant only at centralities over 50%; we can, for a given centrality, take the range of phase shifts determined from different TPC subevents as the uncertainty, and plot the average values across centrality (Fig. 48). The disagreement above 50% centrality is not understood, and follows a similar pattern in this analysis of strange behavior above 50% centrality. Below 50% centrality, there is no observed centrality dependence or TPC subevent dependence of the rotation; it is roughly constant at around 0.058 radians, or about 3 degrees. We could take the average value as the angle by which we correct \(\Psi_{1,{\rm EPD}}\), with its standard deviation of 0.0014 as the systematic uncertainty, but this would be an under-estimation. If we truly want to measure our uncertainty in the value of \(\Delta\Psi_{1,{\rm EPD}}\) then we ought to take the range in uncertainty with the mid-point of the range as the correction; this leaves us with 0.063 \(\pm\) 0.011.

Figure 45: Another way to quantify the rotational effects of \(\vec{B}_{\rm STAR}\) on \(\Psi_{1,{\rm EPD}}\); although we might expect this to look similar to Fig. 47, the observed rotation is smaller. The dependence on TPC subevent is expected because here \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\) effects come into play. TPC subevent 0 covers -1\(<\eta<\)-0.9, subevent 1 covers -0.9\(<\eta<\)-0.8, and so on.

### Polarization observable

#### The restricted invariant-mass method

Absent STAR efficiency corrections, the polarization observable is given by

\[\overline{P}_{\Lambda}=\frac{8}{\pi\alpha_{\Lambda}}\frac{1}{R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}} \left\langle\sin(\Psi_{1}-\phi_{p}^{*})\right\rangle, \tag{24}\]

where \(\alpha_{\Lambda}\) is the \(\Lambda\)'s decay parameter and \(\phi_{p}^{*}\) is the azimuthal angle of the daughter proton in the \(\Lambda\) rest frame at the decay. We know that whenwe reconstruct \(\Lambda\)s, we inevitably have \(p-\pi\) pairs that just happen to pass cuts ("the background"). The background, for various reasons, can have a non-zero \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\), but of course we do not want to include any background polarization in our measurement of \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\). The typical procedure is as follows: First, fit the \(m_{\rm inv.}\) distribution assuming functional forms for the signal and background regions separately (we found a second-degree polynomial for the background and a set of two Gaussian distributions for the signal to work quite well). Second, simply divide the signal fit function by the background fit function and vice versa to get the signal fraction and background fraction as functions of \(m_{\rm inv.}\) (\(f^{\rm sig.}(m_{\rm inv.})\) and \(f^{\rm bgd.}(m_{\rm inv.})\), respectively). Third, plot Eq. 24 vs. \(m_{\rm inv.}\) and fit with the function

\[\overline{P}_{\Lambda}^{\rm obs.}=f^{\rm bgd.}(m_{\rm inv.})\overline{P}_{ \Lambda}^{\rm bgd.}+f^{\rm sig.}(m_{\rm inv.})\overline{P}_{\Lambda}^{\rm sig.} \tag{25}\]

to extract the polarization associated with the true \(\Lambda\)s, \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}^{\rm sig.}\). Though this method has worked in previous measurements with different data sets, there are problems when implementing it here. Fig. 49 demonstrates this method being applied for this data set; we see that, rather than \(\left\langle\sin(\Psi_{1}-\phi_{p}^{*})\right\rangle\) following the form of Eq. 25 as it ought to, it peaks sharply near \(m_{\rm inv.}=m_{\Lambda,\rm PDG}\) and dips sharply on each side of the peak.

Now if we would take the results as they stand here, we would dramatically over-estimate the polarization, almost by a factor of 2; furthermore, we would observe significant dependence of the polarization on \(y\), \(p_{\rm T}\), centrality, etc..

Figure 49: Here, we visually see the procedure known as the invariant-mass method. On the left panel, a second-degree polynomial fits the background (in green) and two summed Gaussian distributions fit the signal (in red). The ratios of these fits give \(f^{\rm sig.}(m_{\rm inv.})\) (in blue) and \(f^{\rm bgd.}(m_{\rm inv.})\) (in black). On the right panel, we see those used to fit \(\left\langle\sin(\Psi_{1}-\phi_{p}^{*})\right\rangle\) with Eq. 25. It is apparent here that the fit function is not appropriate for the data, and that this method is invalid in this situation.

Figure 50: Polarization as a function of rapidity using the standard (and in this case invalid) method; we see dramatic behavior such that the polarization increases strongly with \(y\), and the statistical uncertainties are such that we would quote such a dependence with certainty.

It is clear from Fig. 49 that the standard procedure is, in this case, inappropriate; the measured polarization does not follow the shape of the signal fraction as a function of \(m_{\rm inv.}\). If we were to ignore that fact, we would not only drastically over-estimate the integrated polarization, but we would also measure behavior as a function of \(y\), \(p_{\rm T}\), and centrality that is completely incorrect.

#### The generalized invariant-mass method

We can understand this problem through a combination of a TPC tracking inefficiency (present at all energies) and an asymmetry in the detector-collider system (present only in fixed-target mode) working together to give this effect. Consider two classes of decays: those with \(0<\phi_{\Lambda}-\phi_{p}^{*}<\pi\) and those with \(\pi<\phi_{\Lambda}-\phi_{p}^{*}<2\pi\); a perfect detector would reconstruct such \(\Lambda\)s with equal efficiency, but the STAR TPC can better measure decays with \(0<\phi_{\Lambda}-\phi_{p}^{*}<\pi\). This broken symmetry comes from the STAR magnetic

Figure 52: Polarization as a function of centrality using the standard (and in this case invalid) method; we see dramatic behavior such that the polarization increases strongly with centrality besides a sharp drop at 10-20% centrality, and the statistical uncertainties are such that we would quote such a dependence with certainty.

field, which is along the beam axis (\(\vec{B}_{\rm STAR}=-|\vec{B}_{\rm STAR}|\hat{z}\)); the \(p-\pi\) tracks from \(\Lambda\)s with \(0<\phi_{\Lambda}-\phi_{p}^{*}<\pi\) cross each other in the transverse plane while \(p-\pi\) tracks from \(\Lambda\)s with \(\pi<\phi_{\Lambda}-\phi_{p}^{*}<2\pi\) immediately diverge in the transverse plane after the \(\Lambda\) decay. We can see this illustrated in Figures 53 and 54; keep in mind that \(\vec{B}_{\rm STAR}\) is going into the page so that the proton will curve counter-clockwise and the pion will curve clockwise. The \(m_{\rm inv.}\) distributions differ substantially between these two classes; track-crossing decays apparently have a more sharply peaked \(m_{\rm inv.}\) distribution. We briefly pursued the cause of this effect and found that it may be due to the reconstruction algorithm choosing the decay point by minimizing \(DCA_{p-\pi}\) (which may not be strictly valid), but we leave this for further study; it is not surprising in principle that these decay classes would not have equal efficiencies in the STAR TPC given that the symmetry is broken by \(\vec{B}_{\rm STAR}\). We henceforth refer to this effect as the "Azimuthal Emission Efficiency", or AEE. This AEE (which exists at all energies and in both collider modes) in and of itself would not be a problem; however, in fixed-target mode we have another asymmetry that, when paired with the AEE, invalidates the invariant-mass method described above in the case of fixed-target collisions. Because, in the frame of the STAR detector, one beam is in motion while the other is fixed, the vast majority of tracks measured by the TPC are positive rapidity in the center-of-mass (COM) frame; furthermore, we measure much further into positive rapidity in the COM frame than in collider mode. We therefore have an acceptance- and efficiency-driven correlation between \(\Psi_{1}\) and \(\phi_{\Lambda}\) in fixed-target mode, even when integrated over all measured \(\Lambda\)s, and that correlation is strong because of the strong \(v_{1,\Lambda}\) at this energy; such a correlation, having nothing to do with any sort of physical polarization, could be measured with the very polarization correlator itself: \(\left\langle\sin(\Psi_{1}-\phi_{p}^{*})\right\rangle\).

We can take an idealized scenario to understand all of this clearly. Consider two \(\Lambda\)s with \(\phi_{\Lambda}=\Psi_{1}\) to illustrate the strong \(v_{1,\Lambda}\); one decays with \(\phi_{\Lambda}=\pi\) and the other decays with \(\phi_{\Lambda}=3\pi/2\) to illustrate the two decay classes \(0<\phi_{\Lambda}-\phi_{p}^{*}<\pi\) and \(\pi<\phi_{\Lambda}-\phi_{p}^{*}<2\pi\), respectively. The AEE tells us that the first decay, with \(\phi_{\Lambda}=\pi\), will be more likely to have an \(m_{\rm inv.}\) further from \(m_{\Lambda,{\rm PDG}}\) than the second decay, which in turn will be more likely to have an \(m_{\rm inv.}\) closer to \(m_{\Lambda,{\rm PDG}}\). The first decay will also have \(\left\langle\sin(\Psi_{1}-\phi_{p}^{*})\right\rangle=-1\) while the second will have \(\left\langle\sin(\Psi_{1}-\phi_{p}^{*})\right\rangle=1\); therefore, although the net \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}=0\), we see positive \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) at \(m_{\rm inv.}\approx m_{\Lambda,{\rm PDG}}\) and negative \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) to the sides. This illustration is shown in Fig. 53.

