### Results

Integrated over mid-centrality (20-50%), we measure \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}=4.91\pm 0.82\)(stat.)\(\pm\) 0.15(syst)%. This result is shown in Fig. 61 alongside previous measurements [3, 7, 5, 4], which have been scaled according to the updated value of \(\alpha_{\Lambda}=0.732\)[70]. Also shown are calculations with various models at higher \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\)[37, 59] and recent calculations that extend to small \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\). These include the partonic AMPT model [29], the hadronic UrQMD model [24], and the 3-Fluid Hydrodynamics (3FD) model [31]. Details on the extraction of mid-centrality results from these models can be found in appendix G. While the 3FD and UrQMD models predict a sharply rising \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\) below that of previous measurements, the AMPT model predicts a falling \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\).

We also had sufficient statistics to study \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) differentially. Shown in Fig. 62 is \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) as a function of collision centrality. As was seen in a previous high-statistics data set at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=200\) GeV [50], as well as what is predicted by the various models [24, 29, 31], we observe increasing \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) with collision centrality. What also stands in agreement with this previous measurement as well as the prediction by AMPT is the dependence (or lack thereof) of \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) on \(p_{\rm T}\), shown in Fig. 63.

Figure 61: Statistically significant \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=3\) GeV is measured and plotted alongside previous studies [3, 7, 5, 4] and model predictions [37, 59, 29, 24, 31].

The measurement of the dependence of \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) on \(y\) is uniquely valuable at this collision energy and detector setup because, unlike at higher energies in collider mode, we have access to the most forward-rapidity \(\Lambda\)s. This is significant because many model calculations have predicted a migration of \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) towards forward rapidities [36, 32, 66, 34, 33, 41, 23, 65, 67] and the most forward-rapidity regions have previously remained inaccessible. Ourmeasurement is shown in Fig. 64 and remarkably, we see no dependence of \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) on \(y\) within statistical uncertainties. This stands in stark contrast with model predictions and casts doubt on the argument of vorticity migration towards forward rapidity as an explanation of the dependence of \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) on \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\).

### Summary

This statistically significant measurement of \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=3\) GeV is the highest \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) yet measured, and the lowest collision energy at which it has been measured. The result agrees roughly with predictions made within the context of the 3-Fluid Dynamics model when integrated over \(y\); however, it is dramatically larger than the prediction made within the AMPT framework. Low-energy extensions of the UrQMD study demonstrate striking agreement with our result despite over-estimation at higher energy; this should be taken with a grain of salt, however, as hadronization is drastically over-simplified by a simple scaling of thermal vorticity by 0.5.

The most interesting result obtained is the lack of dependence of \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) on \(y\) within uncertainties. Various models have predicted an enormous dependence [36, 32, 66, 34, 33, 41, 23, 65, 67], and often one that becomes more dramatic as \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\) falls. While future measurements at higher \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\) using the STAR forward upgrade will certainly yield more insight, this data set's observed lack of such a dependence is highly valuable.

## 4 Initial-state fluctuations and angular momentum in heavy-ion collisions

### Introduction

In non-central, high-energy heavy-ion collisions, there exists enormous system angular momentum, \(\vec{J}_{\rm syst}\). Across the energy range spanned by RHIC (\(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=3-200\) GeV), a mid-central (\(b\approx 8\) fm) collision exhibits \(|\vec{J}_{\rm syst}|\sim{\cal O}(10^{5}\hbar)\). For all but the most central collisions (\(b\lesssim 2\) fm), the majority of \(\vec{J}_{\rm syst}\) is transferred to the forward- and backward-going particles, which are dominated by spectator nucleons determined experimentally by \(|y|,|\eta|\gtrsim 2-3\). Figure 65 demonstrates, in the simple Monte-Carlo Glauber model, an estimate of the fraction of total angular momentum carried by the spectators and by the participants, as a function of \(b\).

While spectators carry most of the angular momentum of the system, the remaining \(|\vec{J}_{\rm part}|\) is still significant. The large \(|\vec{J}_{\rm part}|\) is necessary for the collision overlap region, whether there be QGP formation or not, to support vorticity aligned with \(\hat{J}_{\rm part}\). The vorticity, as detailed in section 3, allows us to

