The findings presented hold significant implications for measurements of phenomena driven by angular momentum within the QGP, and particularly for those interested in the dependence on \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\). Based on our model-dependent study, it is crucial for \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\)-dependent studies of angular-momentum-driven phenomena to correct for the decorrelation between \(\hat{J}_{\rm spec}\) and \(\hat{J}_{\rm part}\) in a \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\)-dependent manner, and for model predictions of such phenomena to use \(\hat{J}_{\rm part}\) instead of \(\hat{J}_{\rm syst}\); otherwise any observed dependence will be driven at least in part by this decorrelation.

Summary and future work

Experimental studies of heavy ion collisions require a vast array of detector subsystems, each responsible for specific observables and each with vastly different technology. A crucial observable is that of the collision's orientation, which is estimated by the first-order event-plane angle, \(\Psi_{\rm EP,1}\) determined with the azimuthal distribution of emitted particles at forward rapidity. For over a decade, the BBC served as the primary subsystem within the STAR detector for determining \(\Psi_{\rm EP,1}\); however, its resolution was generally poor owing the the large size of the BBC tiles and the small coverage in \(\eta\).

In 2017-2018 the EPD was constructed as a replacement of the BBC. While many institutions were involved in various stages (including prototyping, construction, software design, etc.), the EPD was constructed and assembled in large part in the Ohio State University's High-Energy Nuclear Physics laboratory within the Department of Physics. Extreme care was required in the various stages of construction and assembly, and unique challenges were regularly confronted. Thorough testing was conducted on each of the twenty-four used plus three spare supersectors using a radioactive source mounted on rails that were moved via program-controlled motors and using cosmic rays. Ultimately, the EPD was successfully installed as the replacement for the BBC and maintains very good performance, and serves as an indispensable tool to measure, for example, the direction of system angular momentum from the collision.

Large system angular momentum, \(\vec{J}_{\rm syst}\), is critical for the generation of vorticity within the collision which leads to the spin alignment of hadrons with \(\hat{J}_{\rm syst}\). This spin polarization has been measured via the decay of the \(\Lambda\) hyperon, which preferentially emits its daughters along the direction of its spin, across a wide range of \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\). The trend strongly suggests that \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) decreases monotonically with \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\). A simple extrapolation of this trend would show \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) becoming very large at small \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\); however, \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) must go to zero at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=2m_{\rm N}\). A peak \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) therefore likely lies in the region \(2m_{\rm N}<\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}<7.7\) GeV, which is the lowest energy at which \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) has yet been measured.

In 2018, the STAR collaboration achieved a high-statistics data set of Au+Au collisions at its lowest-yet center-of-mass collision energy of \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=3\) GeV. Despite these challenges, a measurement of an integrated, mid-rapidity \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) larger than any yet-observed \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) with a statistical significance of \(\approx 6\sigma\) was achieved. Furthermore, the statistics allowed for differential measurements; the measurement of \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) with respect to centrality and \(p_{\rm T}\) confirmed previously observed dependencies, namely that \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) increases with centrality and doesn't depend on \(p_{\rm T}\) within uncertainties. The measurement of \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) with respect to \(y\) was uniquely valuable because the detector setup and collision energy allowed for the measurement of the most-forward \(\Lambda\)s while previous measurements of \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) with respect to \(y\) were constrained to only measure the mid-rapidity \(\Lambda\)s. The lack of any observed dependence of \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) on \(y\) stands in stark contrast to the number of model calculations that predict a rising or falling \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) with \(y\) and to any arguments that the falling \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) with \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\) can be explained by a migration of \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) towards forward rapidity.

Experimental measurements and model calculations of \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) assume that the net vorticity within the collision overlap region, which is parallel to the participant angular momentum \(\vec{J}_{\rm part}\), is parallel to \(\vec{J}_{\rm syst}\). For example, models generate nucleon positions about nuclei centers which exist on the \(\hat{x}\) axis, so that \(\hat{b}||\hat{x}\) and in turn \(\hat{J}_{\rm syst}||-\hat{y}\). Vorticity and polarization vectors are then projected onto \(-\hat{y}\) in order to calculate \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\). Experimentally, the additional assumption is made that the spectator angular momentum, \(\vec{J}_{\rm spec}\), is also parallel to \(\vec{J}_{\rm syst}\) and \(\vec{J}_{\rm part}\); the spectators are measured at forward rapidity (by the EPD for example) and \(\vec{J}_{\rm spec}\) is taken as \(\vec{J}_{\rm part}\) or \(\vec{J}_{\rm syst}\) to be correlated with \(\vec{S}_{\Lambda}\) which is measured at mid-rapidity and is a product of the participant region of the collision.

While these assumptions are true on average, event-by-event fluctuations dictate that they are flawed in any given event. Through the use of a simple Monte-Carlo Glauber model and a more realistic transport model, we quantified the effects of initial-state fluctuations on the correlations between \(\hat{J}_{\rm spec}\), \(\hat{J}_{\rm syst}\), and \(\hat{J}_{\rm part}\). While angular momentum conservation suppresses these correlations slightly, rapidity-window restriction (which is carried out experimentally) and fluctuations have a large effect on these correlations. At lower \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\) the effects are small, but become worse as \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\) becomes larger. While not a significant enough effect to explain the observed \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\) dependence of \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\), this is still a very important corrective factor; if ignored, the \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\) dependence of \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) will be exaggerated. Furthermore, we find that the orientation of the elliptic overlap region is decorrelated with \(\hat{J}_{\rm part}\) which disallows the "escape" of this problem via mid-rapidity particles.

Beyond the importance of understanding the dependence of \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) on \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\)which we've shown is not likely explained by a dependence on \(y\) and is over-estimated due to these event-by-event fluctuations, \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) can be used as a tool for understanding other phenomena. For example, while vorticity within the collision overlap region will polarize \(\Lambda\) and \(\bar{\Lambda}\) hyperons equally, the two have opposite magnetic moments and therefore a given magnetic field sustained by the QGP will enhance \(\overline{P}_{\bar{\Lambda}}\) and suppress \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\). Because of this, a splitting between \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) and \(\overline{P}_{\bar{\Lambda}}\) can be used as a measurement of the magnetic field strength sustained by the QGP and in turn as a measurement of the magnetic susceptibility of the QGP. This process is complicated, however, by other driving mechanisms of such a splitting, such as the difference in emission time from the QGP between \(\Lambda\)s and \(\bar{\Lambda}\)s.

Nevertheless, studying such a splitting between \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) and \(\overline{P}_{\bar{\Lambda}}\) is of much experimental interest. In 2018, STAR collected a high-statistics data set of Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=27\) GeV, motivated in part by this potential splitting. Numerous complicating factors, however, affected this analysis; while a significant effort was put forth in order to account for these complicating factors, the measurements of \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) and \(\overline{P}_{\bar{\Lambda}}\) repeatedly failed checks for systematic errors and sufficient confidence could not be gained that such a charge-sensitive analysis would ultimately be free of unknown systematic effects. Details on this inconclusive study can be found in appendix J. The following year, the STAR collaboration had taken another high-statistics data set at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=19.6\) GeV which included significant detector upgrades and is free of the problems present in the 2018 data set at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=27\) GeV; however, new problems are present. Still, the data set is promising and remains a top priority in my post-graduation research.

