Determination of subevents

### Epd

Momentum-conservation effects are obvious when looking at \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\) vs. centrality (Fig. 92). When using the entire EPD, \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\) remains very large even for peripheral events; however, when dramatically reducing the number of tracks used in \(\Psi_{1}\) determination we see \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\) drop for more peripheral collisions, indicating reduced effects of momentum conservation on the measurement (and, yes, and actual reduction in \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\)).

Figure 92: \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\) for the EPD with no momentum-conservation considerations (i.e. using large subevents that capture a significant fraction of all produced particles. Here, especially at more central events (smaller “centrality bin” is shown), \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\) is noticeably and artificially enhanced.

For our measurement of \(\Psi_{1}\) we use EPD hits from rings 13-16 only (\(-2.9\lesssim\eta\lesssim-2.5\)). For rings 12 and below, with \(\eta\lesssim-2.9\), there is unstable behavior in \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\); we found that the measured \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\) depended strongly on the choice of reference subevents. Also, when looking at \(dN/d\eta\) (Fig. 93), we see an unexpected surplus in yield starting at ring 12. Furthermore, although we know that rapidity and pseudorapidity are not equivalent, we can at least use \(\eta\) as a measure of \(y\); remembering that \(y_{beam}=-2.09\), we may be able to attribute behavior in ring 12 and below to anything "forward" of \(y_{beam}\), which is not sensible for \(\Psi_{1}\) measurement. We also saw jumps in \(\vec{B}_{\rm STAR}\)-driven \(\Psi_{1}\) rotation (elaborated later on) between ring 12 and 13, and the list goes on. Because of its unstable behavior, and because we also don't want a large subevent for \(\Psi_{1}\) calculation (due to momentum-conservation concerns), we are perfectly justified in ignoring EPD rings 12 and below.

In principle, any two groups of particles originating from the same collision will be correlated, at least in part, by momentum-conservation effects. The simplest example is that of two subevents each comprised of one-half of the total emitted particles; the net momentum of one is necessarily equal and opposite the net momentum of the other. As the number of particles in the two subevents approaches zero these effects become negligible. In collider mode, where many particles are produced relative the the number of particles used for subevents, momentum conservation effects are negligible and often ignored; however, at the much lower energy of 3 GeV we must be

