### Tpc

Even without any flow or momentum-conservation effects, \(\Psi_{1}\) measurements between two subevents can be correlated; if subevents contain particles originating from the same "region" of the QGP, then any relevant physics determining the momenta of the particles will necessarily affect both subevents similarly. We of course want our \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\) measurement (determined with multiple subevents) to be free of such non-flow effects and therefore be accurately represented; if that is not possible, then we would at least hope to be able to correct for such a systematic effect or as a last resort fold various measurements into the systematic uncertainties. As discussed earlier, we are more or less set on EPD rings 13-16 as the "\(\Psi_{1}\)-determination subevent"; therefore, the only obvious way to check for non-flow contributions to \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\) is to pick various reference subevent pairs for Eq. 9. In Fig. 97 is shown that, for TPC reference subevents closer in \(\eta\) to the EPD, \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\) becomes artificially large due to non-flow correlations, yet any non-flow correlations between TPC subevents close in \(\eta\) has no effect on \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\) since those contributions are cancelled out (see Eq. 9). We also see that, luckily enough, we are able to find subevents within the TPC far enough from the EPD to minimize these effects; in fact, for TPC subevents 5 and greater (with \(\eta>\)-0.5), there are no observed non-flow contributions to the \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\) measurement (this is highlighted in Fig. 98). Within this handful of reference subevents, we see no pattern as in Fig. 97, so we need not correct or incorporate differences into systematic uncertainties. We also may freely choose any pair of reference subevents, so arbitrarily we choose TPC subevents 5 (-0.5\(<\eta<\)-0.4) and 8 (-0.2\(\eta<\)-0.1).

