Position of EPD tile hit

Typically, when finding any event-plane angle from the EPD, one will use the geometric center of the tile for determining the \(\phi,\eta\) of the corresponding hit(s) (though a non-flat \(dN/d\eta\) distribution means the average hit location will be slightly farther radially from the tile center, the effect would be quite small). At such a low energy, where it is common to have only a small handful of hits in the EPD, we start to see the azimuthal structure of the EPD in the \(\Psi_{1}\) distribution, even after Fourier shifting with 48 terms (which is twice the number of sectors in the EPD). A flat \(\Psi_{1}\) distribution could be achieved by using a random point within the tile for determining the \(\phi\) of the corresponding hit; Fig. 114 shows the Fourier-shifted \(\Psi_{1}\) distributions using these two methods, and it is clear that using a random point within each tile leads to a flat \(\Psi_{1}\) distribution.

One could include more Fourier terms than 48, since any distribution will approach perfect flatness as the number of Fourier terms approaches infinity. Fig. 115 shows these shifting terms out to \(i_{\rm Fourier}=256\), where these shifting terms are still significant. Carrying out shifting to terms in the thousands would be computationally expensive and anyways unnecessary; Fig. 116 shows that there is no preferred direction of \(\Psi_{1}\) in any of the rings and the distribution is sufficiently flat, even when using the tile center. This may seem dubious, but keep in mind that virtually any plotted \(\Psi_{1}\) distribution is hiding similar "spikey" behavior; if we used sufficiently fine binning for the TPC, we would see spikes corresponding to the limits of floating-point precision, and we would use \(\left\langle\sin(\Psi_{1})\right\rangle,\left\langle\cos(\Psi_{1})\right\rangle\) just as in Fig. 116 to justify the non-flat \(\Psi_{1}\) distribution being okay.

The next question might address how the resolutions compare between the two methods. We can simply correlate the \(\Psi_{1}\) measurements between the EPD rings and an arbitrary TPC subevent, and take the ratio between these correlations using the two methods. This is shown in Fig. 117 and, besides the first ring wherein tiles have twice the azimuthal width as any other (and the ratio is anyways still near 1 and, as we'll see later, it has a relatively small \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\)), the two methods yield essentially the same result.

The message of this study is that the choice to use the tile center or a random point within the tile when assigning a \(\phi\) to an EPD hit for the \(\Psi_{1}\) measurement is, more or less, an arbitrary one. Because of this, we arbitrarily choose the one that produces a visually flat distribution (see Fig. 114).

