## Appendix E Efficiency Corrections

### Trigger Efficiency

As in the centrality study, performed by the UC Davis group [60], the efficiency of the trigger to measure when a legitimate collision happened is determined by comparing the measured track-multiplicity distribution to that of a Glauber model (with parameters chosen through fitting). Fig. 118 shows the trigger efficiency as a function of centrality. We simply weight a \(\Lambda\) hyperon's contribution to \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) by the inverse of this trigger efficiency.

Figure 118: Trigger efficiency as a function of collision centrality.

### \(\Lambda\)-measurement efficiency

Due to various factors associated both with the detector system and the \(\Lambda\)-finding algorithm, there is a smaller-than-one probability that a given \(\Lambda\) will be measured, and this probability depends on \(p_{\rm T}\) and \(y\). It is important then to appropriately correct any integrated measurements for this \(\Lambda\)-measurement efficiency. Consider for example a \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) that rises with \(|y|\) and an efficiency that falls with \(|y|\); without any correction, we would under-report contributions to \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) from \(\Lambda\)s with larger \(|y|\) and the integrated \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) measurement would therefore be lower than the true value.

In order to appropriately correct for this effect we want to know the true \(\Lambda\) yield with respect to \(p_{\rm T}\) and \(y\); it may seem impossible to know this, but by using simulations we may generate a given number of \(\Lambda\)s with some arbitrary dependence on \(p_{\rm T}\) and \(y\) (usually flat), embed them into real data (flagging whichever protons and pions came from the embedding), and then reconstruct \(\Lambda\)s as usual. We then simply divide the measured \(\Lambda\) yield by the input \(\Lambda\) yield (as functions of \(p_{\rm T}\) and \(y\)) in order to measure the efficiency. We must take care to use the same exact \(\Lambda\)-finding algorithms (including any kinematic or topological cuts) for these embedded \(\Lambda\)s as we use for the \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) measurement.

For our study, we used an embedded \(\Lambda\) sample provided by Yue-Hang Leung (LBNL); 3M \(\Lambda\)s were embedded with a flat \(p_{\rm T}\) and \(y\) distributions in the range \(0<p_{\rm T}<3\) GeV and \(-2<y_{\rm Lab}<0\). Fig. 119 shows the efficiency calculated for our \(\Lambda\)-finding algorithm.

### Acceptance efficiency

Eq. 24 derives from the global polarization definition,

\[\frac{dN}{d\cos\delta}=\frac{1}{2}\left(1+\alpha_{\rm H}P_{\rm H}\cos\delta \right), \tag{35}\]

where \(\cos\delta=\vec{J}_{\rm sys.}\cdot\hat{p}_{p}^{*}\); however, this derivation assumes that the integral over the solid angle \(d\Omega_{p}^{*}=d\phi_{p}^{*}d\sin\theta_{p}^{*}d\theta_{p}^{*}\) is unaffected by detector acceptance correction. If we appropriately account for this detector acceptance, we instead have

\[\begin{split}\frac{8}{\pi\alpha}\left\langle\sin(\Psi_{1}-\phi_{p }^{*})\right\rangle&=\frac{4}{\pi}\left\langle\sin\theta_{p}^{*} \right\rangle P_{\rm H}-\frac{2}{\pi}\left\langle\sin\theta_{p}^{*}\cos\left[2 \left\langle\phi_{\rm H}-\phi_{p}^{*}\right\rangle\right]\right\rangle P_{\rm H }^{(2)}\\ &=A_{0}P_{\rm H}-A_{2}P_{\rm H}^{(2)}.\end{split} \tag{36}\]

