A search for the magnetic field in the QGP

### Introduction

Global polarization of hadrons emitted by the QGP, \(\overline{P}_{\rm H}\), is measurable via the decay of the \(\Lambda\) hyperon, as it preferentially emits its proton daughter in the direction of its spin. This was measured by the STAR collaboration via the RHIC BES program [7] and subsequently studied in more detail with higher statistics at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=200\) GeV [50] where the dependence of \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) on azimuthal angle \(\phi\), pseudo-rapidity \(\eta\), and transverse momentum \(p_{\rm T}\).

Particularly in the RHIC Beam Energy Scan range of \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\) studied so far, one can see that apparently \(\overline{P}_{\bar{\Lambda}}>\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\), though the difference is not statistically significant (Fig. 36). This splitting becomes more pronounced at lower \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\). One may, by hand, combine the measurements at varying \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\) to obtain an overall splitting of significance \(2\sigma\); enticing, but certainly not convincing. If such a splitting was statistically significant, it could point to a strong, late-stage magnetic field sustained by the QGP and thereby serve as a measure of the magnetic susceptibility of the QGP, \(\chi_{\rm QGP}\)[14, 58], which is of significant theoretical importance [47]. For example, a large magnetic field is a crucial ingredient required to measure the so-called "Chiral Magnetic Effect" which would be evidence of \({\cal C}-{\cal P}\) violation [38].

If one were to take the average enhancement of \(\overline{P}_{\bar{\Lambda}}\) over \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) and extract a magnetic field at face value (despite various potential problems with so doing) according to [14], they would arrive at \(|\vec{B}|_{\rm QGP}=6.0\pm 5.5\cdot 10^{13}\) T, which would be enormous. Such a simple extraction, however, is dubious, as other potential explanations for a splitting between \(\overline{P}_{\bar{\Lambda}}\) and \(\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) exist. One such explanation is the potential difference in origination points between \(\bar{\Lambda}\) and \(\Lambda\) hyperons from within the QGP [61].

Despite these potential problems, it is still worthwhile to study the splitting in a high-statistics data set. In 2018, RHIC/STAR collided Au+Au at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=27\) GeV, collecting sufficient events to study this effect. At this collision energy there is sufficient \(\Lambda\) production, and the ability to resolve the first-order event plane is quite good; therefore it is an ideal data set to look for such an effect.

