### Summary, and search at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=19\) GeV

Although the high-statistics dataset taken at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=27\) GeV was a promising candidate to study \(\overline{P}_{\bar{\Lambda}}-\overline{P}_{\Lambda}\) splitting, there are too many broken symmetries. With such a charge-sensitive analysis, we simply can not guarantee that all systematic effects will be fully accounted for.

Since RHIC/STAR has since taken a high-statistics data set at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=19.6\) GeV, which will be fully calibrated and ready for analysis in roughly two weeks, we will turn our attention there. Initial looks at \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\) at that data set are promising (Fig. 173).

Figure 173: \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\) in uncalibrated \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=19.6\) GeV data; there is a \(15\%\) increase over \(R_{\rm EP}^{(1)}\) seen at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=27\) GeV, with comparable \(\Lambda\) statistics. \(v_{1}\) weighting is achieved on a \(\eta\)-dependent basis (Fig. 174).

